          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What is obtained after removing one hydrogen atom from an alkane is an alkyl group, named by replacing the -ane of the alkane with -yl: methane gives methyl, CH3- (Me-), and ethane gives ethyl, CH3CH2- (Et-) (Table 11.2). For alkanes with more than two carbon atoms, more than one alkyl group can be derived (Table 11.3): from propane, n-propyl (propyl) CH3CH2CH2- and isopropyl (1-methylethyl) CH3CH(CH3)-; from butane, n-butyl (butyl), sec-butyl (1-methylpropyl), isobutyl (2-methylpropyl) and tert-butyl (1,1-dimethylethyl). Butane is the fuel in lighters and isobutane a propellant in shaving gel."
          },
          {
